# =============================================================================
# Caddyfile.standby-upstream.snippet — the ONE upstream change failover makes.
#
# This documents the exact Caddy edit failover.sh performs automatically. Use it
# if you prefer a manual, reviewable edit instead of the in-place sed.
#
# In deploy/caddy/Caddyfile (installed at ~/vaultwarden/caddy/Caddyfile) the two
# reverse_proxy lines point at the PRIMARY app:
#
#     reverse_proxy nextvault:8080
#
# On FAILOVER, change BOTH occurrences (the @admin handle and the catch-all
# handle) to the standby app's container name:
#
#     reverse_proxy nextvault-standby:8080
#
# Then reload Caddy WITHOUT downtime:
#
#     podman exec nextvault-caddy caddy reload --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#
# On FAILBACK, change them back to `nextvault:8080` and reload again.
#
# WHY name-based (not an upstream pool): this is active-PASSIVE. We deliberately
# do NOT list both backends in one `reverse_proxy nextvault:8080 nextvault-standby:8080`
# pool, because that would load-balance writes across TWO app instances against
# (until promotion) different DBs — split brain. Exactly one upstream is live at
# a time; the switch is the failover action. (NIST CP-10.)
#
# Both nextvault and nextvault-standby are on nextvault-internal, so Caddy
# resolves either container name with no network change.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL: health-gated automatic-ish cutover (NOT enabled — keep it manual).
# If you ever want Caddy to stop sending to a dead primary, you could add
# `health_uri /alive` to the reverse_proxy block, but that does NOT promote the
# DB and must never be used to make failover automatic for this control. Manual
# promotion via failover.sh remains the only sanctioned path.
# =============================================================================
